<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Examples for optimal segmentation, best predictor in <math id="S4.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{m,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{m,1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and approximation error curves for a <span id="S4.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">cosine</span> <math id="S4.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and truncated Gaussian <math id="S4.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In (e)-(g), the red markers on the <math id="S4.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-axis denote the optimal segmentation points <math id="S4.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> that were formed from the optimal segment density in (d).</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><span id="S4.F2.2" class="ltx_ERROR ltx_centering undefined">\subfigure</span>
