</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Approximation Error of MoE for Multidimensional Inputs</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now return to the general case of multidimensional inputs, as presented in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Problem and Model Definition ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
While some of the detailed analysis naturally extend the one-dimensional case of Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Approximation Error of MoE for One-Dimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, the multidimensional case has important differences that affect the test error formulation and segmentation optimization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Each region has a volume <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(A_{i})\triangleq\int\displaylimits_{\boldsymbol{x}\in A_{i}}\mathrm{d}\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≜</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(A_{i})\triangleq\int\displaylimits_{\boldsymbol{x}\in A_{i}}\mathrm{d}\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, center <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{i}\triangleq\int\displaylimits_{\boldsymbol{x}\in A_{i}}\boldsymbol{x}\mathrm{d}\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">≜</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{i}\triangleq\int\displaylimits_{\boldsymbol{x}\in A_{i}}\boldsymbol{x}\mathrm{d}\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, and shape that is reflected by the normalized second-moment of inertia about the center <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S5.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(A_{i})\triangleq\frac{\int\displaylimits_{\boldsymbol{x}\in A_{i}}\left\|{{\boldsymbol{x}-\boldsymbol{x}_{i}}}\right\|_{2}^{2}\mathrm{d}\boldsymbol{x}}{d\cdot V(A_{i})^{1+\frac{2}{d}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≜</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(A_{i})\triangleq\frac{\int\displaylimits_{\boldsymbol{x}\in A_{i}}\left\|{{\boldsymbol{x}-\boldsymbol{x}_{i}}}\right\|_{2}^{2}\mathrm{d}\boldsymbol{x}}{d\cdot V(A_{i})^{1+\frac{2}{d}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the normalization makes <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(A_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(A_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> invariant to proportional scaling of a given shape of <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We assume that there are many experts such that their <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional regions <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> are sufficiently small to allow locally-linear approximations of <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, the first-order Taylor approximations of <math id="S5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> around the <math id="S5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\sf th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mi>𝗍𝗁</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\sf th}</annotation></semantics></math> region center <math id="S5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are</p>
<table id="S5.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\beta(\boldsymbol{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\beta(\boldsymbol{x})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E25.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\beta(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})+\nabla\beta(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})^{T}\left(\boldsymbol{x}-\boldsymbol{x}_{i}\right)+R_{\beta,1}(\boldsymbol{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>β</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\beta(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})+\nabla\beta(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})^{T}\left(\boldsymbol{x}-\boldsymbol{x}_{i}\right)+R_{\beta,1}(\boldsymbol{x})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{\mathbf{x}}(\boldsymbol{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{\mathbf{x}}(\boldsymbol{x})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E26.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=p_{\mathbf{x}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})+\nabla p_{\mathbf{x}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})^{T}\left(\boldsymbol{x}-\boldsymbol{x}_{i}\right)+R_{p_{\mathbf{x}},1}(\boldsymbol{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=p_{\mathbf{x}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})+\nabla p_{\mathbf{x}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})^{T}\left(\boldsymbol{x}-\boldsymbol{x}_{i}\right)+R_{p_{\mathbf{x}},1}(\boldsymbol{x})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\beta(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\beta(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla p_{\mathbf{x}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla p_{\mathbf{x}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> are the gradients of <math id="S5.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, at the region center <math id="S5.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S5.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\beta,1}\in o\left(\left\|{{\boldsymbol{x}-\boldsymbol{x}_{i}}}\right\|_{2}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\beta,1}\in o\left(\left\|{{\boldsymbol{x}-\boldsymbol{x}_{i}}}\right\|_{2}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{p_{\mathbf{x}},1}\in o\left(\left\|{{\boldsymbol{x}-\boldsymbol{x}_{i}}}\right\|_{2}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{p_{\mathbf{x}},1}\in o\left(\left\|{{\boldsymbol{x}-\boldsymbol{x}_{i}}}\right\|_{2}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> are remainder terms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Using the locally-linear approximations (<a href="#S5.E25" title="In 5 Approximation Error of MoE for Multidimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>)-(<a href="#S5.E26" title="In 5 Approximation Error of MoE for Multidimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>), the optimal expert constants (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3 Problem and Model Definition ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) can be formulated and their test error (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3 Problem and Model Definition ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) can be upper bounded as follows (see proof in Appendix <a href="#A6.SS1" title="F.1 Proof of Theorem : The Optimal Constants and Test Error in Sum Form using Locally-Linear Approximations for Multidimensional Inputs ‣ Appendix F Proofs for the Case of Multidimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a given segmentation of <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> regions <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, the optimal expert constants are</span></p>
<table id="S5.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}^{\sf opt}=\beta(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})+o\left(V(A_{i})^{1/d}\right)~~\text{as}~V(A_{i})\rightarrow 0,~~\forall i\in\{1,\dots,m\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>𝗈𝗉𝗍</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.660em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">as</mtext><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.827em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}^{\sf opt}=\beta(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})+o\left(V(A_{i})^{1/d}\right)~~\text{as}~V(A_{i})\rightarrow 0,~~\forall i\in\{1,\dots,m\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The corresponding test error can be upper bounded as</span></p>
<table id="S5.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\sf test}\left({\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m},\{c_{i}^{\sf opt}\}_{i=1}^{m}}\right)\leq\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}+d\sum_{i=1}^{m}{\left\|{{\nabla\beta(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})}}\right\|_{2}^{2}p_{\mathbf{x}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})M(A_{i})V(A_{i})^{1+\frac{2}{d}}+o\left({V_{\sf max}^{1+\frac{2}{d}}}\right)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>𝗍𝖾𝗌𝗍</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>𝗈𝗉𝗍</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>𝗆𝖺𝗑</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\sf test}\left({\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m},\{c_{i}^{\sf opt}\}_{i=1}^{m}}\right)\leq\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}+d\sum_{i=1}^{m}{\left\|{{\nabla\beta(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})}}\right\|_{2}^{2}p_{\mathbf{x}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})M(A_{i})V(A_{i})^{1+\frac{2}{d}}+o\left({V_{\sf max}^{1+\frac{2}{d}}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(A_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(A_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is the normalized second-moment of inertia of <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\sf max}=\underset{i\in\{1,\dots,m\}}{\max}V(A_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>𝗆𝖺𝗑</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\sf max}=\underset{i\in\{1,\dots,m\}}{\max}V(A_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is the largest region volume.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The test error on the left side in (<a href="#S5.E28" title="In Theorem 4. ‣ 5 Approximation Error of MoE for Multidimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>) is the approximation error <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\sf app}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>𝖺𝗉𝗉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\sf app}</annotation></semantics></math> of the hypothesis class <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{m,d}^{c}\left(\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{m,d}^{c}\left(\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>; this is due to the definitions in (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3 Problem and Model Definition ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In 3 Problem and Model Definition ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) and the proof of (<a href="#S5.E28" title="In Theorem 4. ‣ 5 Approximation Error of MoE for Multidimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>) where the optimal constants are set in the test error formula <span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">before</span> developing the upper bound.
Importantly, note that (<a href="#S5.E28" title="In Theorem 4. ‣ 5 Approximation Error of MoE for Multidimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>) is an upper bound on the test error and not formulation with equality as in the one-dimensional input case (<a href="#S4.E19" title="In Theorem 1. ‣ 4.1 Small Subintervals Assumption and Locally-Linear Approximations ‣ 4 Approximation Error of MoE for One-Dimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>); this is due to the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality applications on the inner products of gradients and centered input vectors, for more details see (<a href="#A6.E99" title="In F.1 Proof of Theorem : The Optimal Constants and Test Error in Sum Form using Locally-Linear Approximations for Multidimensional Inputs ‣ Appendix F Proofs for the Case of Multidimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">99</span></a>)-(<a href="#A6.Ex28" title="In F.1 Proof of Theorem : The Optimal Constants and Test Error in Sum Form using Locally-Linear Approximations for Multidimensional Inputs ‣ Appendix F Proofs for the Case of Multidimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">101</span></a>) in the proof (Appendix <a href="#A6.SS1" title="F.1 Proof of Theorem : The Optimal Constants and Test Error in Sum Form using Locally-Linear Approximations for Multidimensional Inputs ‣ Appendix F Proofs for the Case of Multidimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by high-rate vector quantization theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gersho, 1979</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Na and Neuhoff, 1995</a>)</cite>, we define a density function for the input-space segments of experts in our multidimensional problem (extending Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 4.2 Optimizing the Routing Segmentation using Expert Segment Density ‣ 4 Approximation Error of MoE for One-Dimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for one-dimensional inputs).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 2</span></span><span id="Thmassumption2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists an expert segment density function <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda:[0,1]^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda:[0,1]^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> which is smooth and satisfies <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(\boldsymbol{x})\approx\frac{1}{mV(A_{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(\boldsymbol{x})\approx\frac{1}{mV(A_{i})}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}\in A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}\in A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, at the region centers, <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})\approx\frac{1}{mV(A_{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})\approx\frac{1}{mV(A_{i})}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(A_{i})\approx\frac{1}{m\lambda(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(A_{i})\approx\frac{1}{m\lambda(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall i\in\{1,\dots,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall i\in\{1,\dots,m\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The multidimensional case requires to consider the segmentation region shapes, and specifically their normalized moment of inertia <math id="S5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{M(A_{i})\}_{i=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{M(A_{i})\}_{i=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> that appear in (<a href="#S5.E28" title="In Theorem 4. ‣ 5 Approximation Error of MoE for Multidimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>).
Similarly to the vector quantization analysis by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Na and Neuhoff (1995)</a></cite>, the following assumption will be analytically useful.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3</span></span><span id="Thmassumption3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a smooth function <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu:[0,1]^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu:[0,1]^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, known as the normalized moment of inertia profile, that satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S5.E29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\boldsymbol{x})\approx M(A_{i})~~\text{for}~\boldsymbol{x}\in A_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.660em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for</mtext><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\boldsymbol{x})\approx M(A_{i})~~\text{for}~\boldsymbol{x}\in A_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmassumption3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Therefore, at the region centers, <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})\approx M(A_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})\approx M(A_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall i\in\{1,\dots,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall i\in\{1,\dots,m\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Using Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2. ‣ 5 Approximation Error of MoE for Multidimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>-<a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3. ‣ 5 Approximation Error of MoE for Multidimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and approximating the sum in (<a href="#S5.E28" title="In Theorem 4. ‣ 5 Approximation Error of MoE for Multidimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>) as integral due to the small segment assumption, we get an approximated formula for the error upper-bound as corollary of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4. ‣ 5 Approximation Error of MoE for Multidimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The upper bound on the test error for optimal constants can be approximated using continuous integration: <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\sf test}\leq\mathcal{E}_{\sf test}^{\sf bound}\approx\widetilde{\mathcal{E}}_{\sf test}^{\sf bound}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>𝗍𝖾𝗌𝗍</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>𝗍𝖾𝗌𝗍</mi><mi>𝖻𝗈𝗎𝗇𝖽</mi></msubsup><mo>≈</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>𝗍𝖾𝗌𝗍</mi><mi>𝖻𝗈𝗎𝗇𝖽</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\sf test}\leq\mathcal{E}_{\sf test}^{\sf bound}\approx\widetilde{\mathcal{E}}_{\sf test}^{\sf bound}</annotation></semantics></math> where</span></p>
<table id="S5.E30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{E}}_{\sf test}^{\sf bound}=\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}+\frac{d}{m^{2/d}}\int_{\boldsymbol{x}\in[0,1]^{d}}{\frac{\left\|{{\nabla\beta(\boldsymbol{x})}}\right\|_{2}^{2}p_{\mathbf{x}}(\boldsymbol{x})\mu(\boldsymbol{x})}{\lambda^{2/d}(\boldsymbol{x})}\mathrm{d}\boldsymbol{x}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>𝗍𝖾𝗌𝗍</mi><mi>𝖻𝗈𝗎𝗇𝖽</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{E}}_{\sf test}^{\sf bound}=\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}+\frac{d}{m^{2/d}}\int_{\boldsymbol{x}\in[0,1]^{d}}{\frac{\left\|{{\nabla\beta(\boldsymbol{x})}}\right\|_{2}^{2}p_{\mathbf{x}}(\boldsymbol{x})\mu(\boldsymbol{x})}{\lambda^{2/d}(\boldsymbol{x})}\mathrm{d}\boldsymbol{x}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\boldsymbol{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\boldsymbol{x})</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth moment of inertia profile from Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3. ‣ 5 Approximation Error of MoE for Multidimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p9.1" class="ltx_p">More details on (<a href="#S5.E30" title="In Corollary 5. ‣ 5 Approximation Error of MoE for Multidimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></a>) are provided in Appendix <a href="#A6.SS2" title="F.2 Additional Details on Corollary ‣ Appendix F Proofs for the Case of Multidimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that the volume of the <math id="S5.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional unit cube is 1, therefore cases where the value of <math id="S5.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\left\|{{\nabla\beta(\boldsymbol{x})}}\right\|_{2}^{2}p_{\mathbf{x}}(\boldsymbol{x})\mu(\boldsymbol{x})}{\lambda^{2/d}(\boldsymbol{x})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\left\|{{\nabla\beta(\boldsymbol{x})}}\right\|_{2}^{2}p_{\mathbf{x}}(\boldsymbol{x})\mu(\boldsymbol{x})}{\lambda^{2/d}(\boldsymbol{x})}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}\in[0,1]^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}\in[0,1]^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> can be upper bounded by a constant agnostic of <math id="S5.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> motivate the following assumption.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 4</span></span><span id="Thmassumption4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmassumption4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{\boldsymbol{x}\in[0,1]^{d}}{\frac{\left\|{{\nabla\beta(\boldsymbol{x})}}\right\|_{2}^{2}p_{\mathbf{x}}(\boldsymbol{x})\mu(\boldsymbol{x})}{\lambda^{2/d}(\boldsymbol{x})}\mathrm{d}\boldsymbol{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{\boldsymbol{x}\in[0,1]^{d}}{\frac{\left\|{{\nabla\beta(\boldsymbol{x})}}\right\|_{2}^{2}p_{\mathbf{x}}(\boldsymbol{x})\mu(\boldsymbol{x})}{\lambda^{2/d}(\boldsymbol{x})}\mathrm{d}\boldsymbol{x}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmassumption4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> can be upper bounded by a constant agnostic of <math id="Thmassumption4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmassumption4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Then, Corollary <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Corollary 5. ‣ 5 Approximation Error of MoE for Multidimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> implies the following big-O bound on the approximation error.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 6</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption4" title="Assumption 4. ‣ 5 Approximation Error of MoE for Multidimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, the approximation error satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S5.E31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\sf app}\left(\mathcal{H}_{m,d}^{c}\left(\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}\right)\right)=\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}+\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{d}{m^{2/d}}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>𝖺𝗉𝗉</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\sf app}\left(\mathcal{H}_{m,d}^{c}\left(\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}\right)\right)=\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}+\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{d}{m^{2/d}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is a discrete segmentation that theoretically corresponds to a given segment density <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(\boldsymbol{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(\boldsymbol{x})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p12.1" class="ltx_p">In Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C MoE for Multidimensional Inputs: The Expert Segment Density that Minimizes the Test-Error Upper Bound ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>, we continue to analyze the minimization of the upper bound by the segmentation — although the bound of Corollary <a href="#Thmtheorem6" title="Corollary 6. ‣ 5 Approximation Error of MoE for Multidimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> is sufficient for analyzing the statistical learning with <math id="S5.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{m,d}^{c}\left(\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{m,d}^{c}\left(\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> in the next section.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Learning the Constant Experts: A Tradeoff in the Number of Experts</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The previous sections studied the approximation error of the MoE model classes (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3 Problem and Model Definition ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3 Problem and Model Definition ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), namely, the best performance that these model classes can achieve for a given data distribution (i.e., known input and noise distributions and the true <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> that defines the output distribution).
Now, we turn to study the generalization aspect of learning the expert constants from training examples (and without knowing the true data distribution) for our MoE model with a given routing segmentation of the input space.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Learning Expert Constants via Least Squares</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a training dataset of <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> input-output examples <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{n}\triangleq\left\{\left(\boldsymbol{x}^{({j})},y^{({j})}\right)\right\}_{j=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≜</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{n}\triangleq\left\{\left(\boldsymbol{x}^{({j})},y^{({j})}\right)\right\}_{j=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. drawn from the data model (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3 Problem and Model Definition ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). The <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{\sf th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>j</mi><mi>𝗍𝗁</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{\sf th}</annotation></semantics></math> training example satisfies the data model</p>
<table id="S6.E32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{({j})}=\beta\left(\boldsymbol{x}^{({j})}\right)+\epsilon^{({j})}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{({j})}=\beta\left(\boldsymbol{x}^{({j})}\right)+\epsilon^{({j})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{({j})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{({j})}</annotation></semantics></math> is the underlying noise component.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Importantly, now we do not know the true <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> nor the input PDF <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, therefore computation of the optimal expert constants directly via (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3 Problem and Model Definition ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) or (<a href="#S5.E27" title="In Theorem 4. ‣ 5 Approximation Error of MoE for Multidimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>) is impossible. Instead, we learn the expert constants for a given routing segmentation <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. This corresponds to learning a model from the hypothesis class <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{m,d}^{c}\left(\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{m,d}^{c}\left(\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, as defined in (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3 Problem and Model Definition ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We define <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}^{({j})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}^{({j})}</annotation></semantics></math> as an indicator for the inclusion of the <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{\sf th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>j</mi><mi>𝗍𝗁</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{\sf th}</annotation></semantics></math> training input <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}^{({j})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}^{({j})}</annotation></semantics></math> in the <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\sf th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mi>𝗍𝗁</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\sf th}</annotation></semantics></math> region <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S6.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E33.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q_{i}^{({j})}\triangleq\mathbb{I}\left[{\boldsymbol{x}^{({j})}\in A_{i}}\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≜</mo><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}^{({j})}\triangleq\mathbb{I}\left[{\boldsymbol{x}^{({j})}\in A_{i}}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}\left[{\sf condition}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>𝖼𝗈𝗇𝖽𝗂𝗍𝗂𝗈𝗇</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}\left[{\sf condition}\right]</annotation></semantics></math> returns 1 if its <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sf condition}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖼𝗈𝗇𝖽𝗂𝗍𝗂𝗈𝗇</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sf condition}</annotation></semantics></math> is satisfied, and 0 otherwise.
Accordingly, the number of training examples that are routed to the <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\sf th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mi>𝗍𝗁</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\sf th}</annotation></semantics></math> expert is</p>
<table id="S6.E34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}\triangleq\sum_{j=1}^{n}q_{i}^{({j})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">≜</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}\triangleq\sum_{j=1}^{n}q_{i}^{({j})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Then, for a given routing segmentation <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\sf th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mi>𝗍𝗁</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\sf th}</annotation></semantics></math> expert constant is learned via least squares</p>
<table id="S6.E35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E35.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\forall i\in\{1,\dots,m\}~\text{such that}~~n_{i}&gt;0,~~~~\widetilde{c}_{i}=\underset{v\in\mathbb{R}}{\argmin}\sum_{j:q_{i}^{({j})}=1}\left({v-y^{({j})}}\right)^{2}=\frac{1}{n_{i}}\sum_{j:q_{i}^{({j})}=1}y^{({j})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo rspace="0.330em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mtext>such that</mtext><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="1.487em">,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">v</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">ℝ</mi></mrow></munder><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></munder><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall i\in\{1,\dots,m\}~\text{such that}~~n_{i}&gt;0,~~~~\widetilde{c}_{i}=\underset{v\in\mathbb{R}}{\argmin}\sum_{j:q_{i}^{({j})}=1}\left({v-y^{({j})}}\right)^{2}=\frac{1}{n_{i}}\sum_{j:q_{i}^{({j})}=1}y^{({j})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.4" class="ltx_p">Note that the <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\sf th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mi>𝗍𝗁</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\sf th}</annotation></semantics></math> expert constant is learned only from the examples that are routed to it. As we consider here a fixed, non-learned input-space segmentation, there is some probability that a region might not get training examples (i.e., <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>); in such regions, one may apply a globally default value for the expert, for example, if <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}=0</annotation></semantics></math> set the average of all outputs in the training dataset <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{c}_{i}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}y^{({j})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{c}_{i}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}y^{({j})}</annotation></semantics></math>. This will be useful for the empirical analysis in Appendix <a href="#S6.SS3" title="6.3 Empirical Results: Learning Experts from Training Data for MoE with Uniform Segmentation for One-Dimensional Inputs ‣ 6 Learning the Constant Experts: A Tradeoff in the Number of Experts ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>. The theoretical analysis below will focus on the main case of (<a href="#S6.E35" title="In 6.1 Learning Expert Constants via Least Squares ‣ 6 Learning the Constant Experts: A Tradeoff in the Number of Experts ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The following theorem shows that the learned expert constant <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{c}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{c}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for unknown <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> (and <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>) is an unbiased estimate of the optimal expert constant <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}^{\sf opt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>𝗈𝗉𝗍</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}^{\sf opt}</annotation></semantics></math> for known <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>. The proof is in Appendix <a href="#A7.SS1" title="G.1 Proof of Theorem : The Learned Constant Expert is Unbiased ‣ Appendix G Proofs for Section ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.1</span></a></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The expectation of the expert constant <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{c}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{c}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> learned via (<a href="#S6.E35" title="In 6.1 Learning Expert Constants via Least Squares ‣ 6 Learning the Constant Experts: A Tradeoff in the Number of Experts ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>) given <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> training examples is the optimal expert constant <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}^{\sf opt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>𝗈𝗉𝗍</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}^{\sf opt}</annotation></semantics></math>:    <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[{{\widetilde{c}_{i}|n_{i}&gt;0}}\right]=c_{i}^{\sf opt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>𝗈𝗉𝗍</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[{{\widetilde{c}_{i}|n_{i}&gt;0}}\right]=c_{i}^{\sf opt}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>Test Error Analysis for MoE with Learned Expert Constants</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We start the analysis by decomposing the test error of the MoE with learned constants. The probability of the input routed to the <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\sf th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mi>𝗍𝗁</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\sf th}</annotation></semantics></math> expert is denoted as</p>
<table id="S6.E36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E36.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\rho_{i}\triangleq\Prob\left[{\mathbf{x}\in A_{i}}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≜</mo><mi>Prob</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{i}\triangleq\Prob\left[{\mathbf{x}\in A_{i}}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(36)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 8</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a given routing segmentation <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, the test error of MoE with learned constants <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{\widetilde{c}_{i}\right\}_{i=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\widetilde{c}_{i}\right\}_{i=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> can be decomposed as</span></p>
<table id="S6.E37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\sf test}\left({\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m},\left\{\widetilde{c}_{i}\right\}_{i=1}^{m}}\right)=\mathcal{E}_{\sf app}\left(\mathcal{H}_{m,d}^{c}\left(\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}\right)\right)+\mathcal{E}_{\sf est}(m,\mathcal{S}_{n})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>𝗍𝖾𝗌𝗍</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>𝖺𝗉𝗉</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>𝖾𝗌𝗍</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\sf test}\left({\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m},\left\{\widetilde{c}_{i}\right\}_{i=1}^{m}}\right)=\mathcal{E}_{\sf app}\left(\mathcal{H}_{m,d}^{c}\left(\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}\right)\right)+\mathcal{E}_{\sf est}(m,\mathcal{S}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(37)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the approximation error equals the test error of MoE with optimal constants <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{c_{i}^{\sf opt}\right\}_{i=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>𝗈𝗉𝗍</mi></msubsup><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{c_{i}^{\sf opt}\right\}_{i=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</span></p>
<table id="S6.E38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\sf app}\left(\mathcal{H}_{m,d}^{c}\left(\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}\right)\right)=\mathcal{E}_{\sf test}\left({\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m},\left\{c_{i}^{\sf opt}\right\}_{i=1}^{m}}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>𝖺𝗉𝗉</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>𝗍𝖾𝗌𝗍</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>𝗈𝗉𝗍</mi></msubsup><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\sf app}\left(\mathcal{H}_{m,d}^{c}\left(\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}\right)\right)=\mathcal{E}_{\sf test}\left({\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m},\left\{c_{i}^{\sf opt}\right\}_{i=1}^{m}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(38)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem8.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and the estimation error, which reflects the statistical learning of <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> expert constants from the training dataset <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, is formulated as</span></p>
<table id="S6.E39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\sf est}(m,\mathcal{S}_{n})=\sum_{i=1}^{m}\left(\widetilde{c}_{i}-c_{i}^{\sf opt}\right)^{2}\rho_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>𝖾𝗌𝗍</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>𝗈𝗉𝗍</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\sf est}(m,\mathcal{S}_{n})=\sum_{i=1}^{m}\left(\widetilde{c}_{i}-c_{i}^{\sf opt}\right)^{2}\rho_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(39)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">See proof in Appendix <a href="#A7.SS2" title="G.2 Proof of Lemma ‣ Appendix G Proofs for Section ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.2</span></a>.
This theorem decomposes the MoE test error into approximation error and estimation error, a decomposition which is widely used in machine learning theory (see, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shalev-Shwartz and Ben-David, 2014</a>)</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The test error expression in Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Lemma 8. ‣ 6.2 Test Error Analysis for MoE with Learned Expert Constants ‣ 6 Learning the Constant Experts: A Tradeoff in the Number of Experts ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> motivates us to analyze the statistical behavior of the distance <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lvert\widetilde{c}_{i}-c_{i}^{\sf opt}\right\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>𝗈𝗉𝗍</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lvert\widetilde{c}_{i}-c_{i}^{\sf opt}\right\rvert</annotation></semantics></math> between the learned constants and their optimal counterparts. First, let us formulate concentration bounds for the number <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of training examples that are routed to the <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\sf th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mi>𝗍𝗁</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\sf th}</annotation></semantics></math> expert.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 9</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\delta}\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\delta}\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and given <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq\frac{8}{\rho_{i}}\ln{\left(\frac{1}{\widetilde{\delta}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>8</mn><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq\frac{8}{\rho_{i}}\ln{\left(\frac{1}{\widetilde{\delta}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Prob\left[{n_{i}&gt;\frac{1}{2}n\rho_{i}}\right]\geq 1-\widetilde{\delta}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Prob</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Prob\left[{n_{i}&gt;\frac{1}{2}n\rho_{i}}\right]\geq 1-\widetilde{\delta}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The proof uses the Chernoff bound for a sum of independent Bernoulli variables; see Appendix <a href="#A7.SS3" title="G.3 Proof of Lemma ‣ Appendix G Proofs for Section ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Then, using Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma 9. ‣ 6.2 Test Error Analysis for MoE with Learned Expert Constants ‣ 6 Learning the Constant Experts: A Tradeoff in the Number of Experts ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> and assuming the noise <math id="S6.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> in the data model (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3 Problem and Model Definition ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) is from a bounded range, the distance <math id="S6.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lvert\widetilde{c}_{i}-c_{i}^{\sf opt}\right\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>𝗈𝗉𝗍</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lvert\widetilde{c}_{i}-c_{i}^{\sf opt}\right\rvert</annotation></semantics></math> is statistically bounded as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 10</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider the <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\sf th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mi>𝗍𝗁</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\sf th}</annotation></semantics></math> region <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose value range size of <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted as <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\beta,i}\triangleq\underset{\boldsymbol{x}\in A_{i}}{\max}\beta(\boldsymbol{x})-\underset{\boldsymbol{x}\in A_{i}}{\min}\beta(\boldsymbol{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≜</mo><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">A</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">A</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\beta,i}\triangleq\underset{\boldsymbol{x}\in A_{i}}{\max}\beta(\boldsymbol{x})-\underset{\boldsymbol{x}\in A_{i}}{\min}\beta(\boldsymbol{x})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume a positive probability <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{i}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{i}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> for the input routed to <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume the noise <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is from the bounded range <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\epsilon_{\sf min},\epsilon_{\sf max}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>𝗆𝗂𝗇</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>𝗆𝖺𝗑</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\epsilon_{\sf min},\epsilon_{\sf max}]</annotation></semantics></math> and denote <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\epsilon}\triangleq\epsilon_{\sf max}-\epsilon_{\sf min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo>≜</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>𝗆𝖺𝗑</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>𝗆𝗂𝗇</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\epsilon}\triangleq\epsilon_{\sf max}-\epsilon_{\sf min}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem10.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem10.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem10.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, for </span><math id="Thmtheorem10.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem10.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="Thmtheorem10.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\delta}\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\delta}\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem10.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and given </span><math id="Thmtheorem10.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq\frac{8}{\rho_{i}}\ln{\left(\frac{1}{\widetilde{\delta}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>8</mn><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq\frac{8}{\rho_{i}}\ln{\left(\frac{1}{\widetilde{\delta}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem10.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
<table id="S6.E40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lvert\widetilde{c}_{i}-c_{i}^{\sf opt}\right\rvert&lt;\gamma\frac{R_{\beta,i}+R_{\epsilon}}{\sqrt{n\rho_{i}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>𝗈𝗉𝗍</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lvert\widetilde{c}_{i}-c_{i}^{\sf opt}\right\rvert&lt;\gamma\frac{R_{\beta,i}+R_{\epsilon}}{\sqrt{n\rho_{i}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(40)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem10.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem10.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with probability at least </span><math id="Thmtheorem10.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-2\exp\left(-\gamma^{2}\right)-\widetilde{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-2\exp\left(-\gamma^{2}\right)-\widetilde{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem10.p2.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The proof is provided in Appendix <a href="#A7.SS4" title="G.4 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix G Proofs for Section ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Next, using Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Lemma 8. ‣ 6.2 Test Error Analysis for MoE with Learned Expert Constants ‣ 6 Learning the Constant Experts: A Tradeoff in the Number of Experts ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 10. ‣ 6.2 Test Error Analysis for MoE with Learned Expert Constants ‣ 6 Learning the Constant Experts: A Tradeoff in the Number of Experts ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, the test error of MoE with learned constants <math id="S6.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{\widetilde{c}_{i}\right\}_{i=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\widetilde{c}_{i}\right\}_{i=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> can be upper bounded as follows (proof in Appendix <a href="#A7.SS5" title="G.5 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix G Proofs for Section ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 11</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a given routing segmentation <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{i}&gt;0~\forall i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.497em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{i}&gt;0~\forall i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\delta}\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\delta}\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, and given <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq\frac{8}{\underset{i\in\{1,\dots,m\}}{\min}\rho_{i}}\ln{\left(\frac{1}{\widetilde{\delta}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>8</mn><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.710em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq\frac{8}{\underset{i\in\{1,\dots,m\}}{\min}\rho_{i}}\ln{\left(\frac{1}{\widetilde{\delta}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S6.E41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\sf est}(m,\mathcal{S}_{n})&lt;\frac{m}{n}\gamma^{2}\underset{i\in\{1,\dots,m\}}{\max}\left(R_{\beta,i}+R_{\epsilon}\right)^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>𝖾𝗌𝗍</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\sf est}(m,\mathcal{S}_{n})&lt;\frac{m}{n}\gamma^{2}\underset{i\in\{1,\dots,m\}}{\max}\left(R_{\beta,i}+R_{\epsilon}\right)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(41)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem11.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem11.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with probability at least <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-2m\exp\left(-\gamma^{2}\right)-m\widetilde{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-2m\exp\left(-\gamma^{2}\right)-m\widetilde{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The upper bound on the estimation error in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem11" title="Theorem 11. ‣ 6.2 Test Error Analysis for MoE with Learned Expert Constants ‣ 6 Learning the Constant Experts: A Tradeoff in the Number of Experts ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> implies <math id="S6.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\sf est}(m,\mathcal{S}_{n})\in\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m}{n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>𝖾𝗌𝗍</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\sf est}(m,\mathcal{S}_{n})\in\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m}{n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Corollary <a href="#Thmtheorem6" title="Corollary 6. ‣ 5 Approximation Error of MoE for Multidimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we already know that the approximation error behaves as <math id="S6.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\sf app}\left(\mathcal{H}_{m,d}^{c}\left(\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}\right)\right)=\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}+\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{d}{m^{2/d}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>𝖺𝗉𝗉</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\sf app}\left(\mathcal{H}_{m,d}^{c}\left(\{A_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}\right)\right)=\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}+\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{d}{m^{2/d}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, by the test error decomposition (<a href="#S6.E37" title="In Lemma 8. ‣ 6.2 Test Error Analysis for MoE with Learned Expert Constants ‣ 6 Learning the Constant Experts: A Tradeoff in the Number of Experts ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">37</span></a>), there is a tradeoff in the test error as function of the number of experts <math id="S6.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>.
Considering a fixed input dimension <math id="S6.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and a fixed dataset size <math id="S6.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the tradeoff is based on two contradicting trends: For MoE with more experts, i.e., increasing <math id="S6.SS2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<ul id="S6.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the MoE expressiveness improves as reflected by reduction in the approximation error as <math id="S6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{d}{m^{2/d}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{d}{m^{2/d}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:-5.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the statistical learning degrades, as each expert is trained on less examples out of the <math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> training examples, leading to estimation error that increases as <math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m}{n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m}{n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S6.SS2.p8.2" class="ltx_p">This theoretical tradeoff is qualitatively supported by our empirical results for one-dimensional inputs in the following subsection.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3 </span>Empirical Results: Learning Experts from Training Data for MoE with Uniform Segmentation for One-Dimensional Inputs</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now we proceed to experiments of learning the constant experts from a given training dataset. Similarly to Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Learning the Constant Experts: A Tradeoff in the Number of Experts ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, the considered MoE is for a given segmentation (which is not necessarily optimal) that does not need to be learned; here the segmentation will be uniform, i.e., <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}=\frac{i}{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}=\frac{i}{m}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,\dots,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,\dots,m\}</annotation></semantics></math> for the one-dimensional MoE class <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{m,1}^{c}\left(\{a_{i}=\frac{i}{m}\}_{i=1}^{m}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{m,1}^{c}\left(\{a_{i}=\frac{i}{m}\}_{i=1}^{m}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, as was more generally defined in (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3 Problem and Model Definition ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The learning of the expert constants is by the region-based least squares solution in (<a href="#S6.E35" title="In 6.1 Learning Expert Constants via Least Squares ‣ 6 Learning the Constant Experts: A Tradeoff in the Number of Experts ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>), and with the exception that expert constants of regions without training examples are set as the average of all outputs in the training dataset.
The learning experiment results are provided in Figs. <a href="#S6.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 6.3 Empirical Results: Learning Experts from Training Data for MoE with Uniform Segmentation for One-Dimensional Inputs ‣ 6 Learning the Constant Experts: A Tradeoff in the Number of Experts ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>-<a href="#S6.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 6.3 Empirical Results: Learning Experts from Training Data for MoE with Uniform Segmentation for One-Dimensional Inputs ‣ 6 Learning the Constant Experts: A Tradeoff in the Number of Experts ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
Figs. <a href="#S6.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 6.3 Empirical Results: Learning Experts from Training Data for MoE with Uniform Segmentation for One-Dimensional Inputs ‣ 6 Learning the Constant Experts: A Tradeoff in the Number of Experts ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>-<a href="#S6.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 6.3 Empirical Results: Learning Experts from Training Data for MoE with Uniform Segmentation for One-Dimensional Inputs ‣ 6 Learning the Constant Experts: A Tradeoff in the Number of Experts ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <a href="#S6.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 6.3 Empirical Results: Learning Experts from Training Data for MoE with Uniform Segmentation for One-Dimensional Inputs ‣ 6 Learning the Constant Experts: A Tradeoff in the Number of Experts ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>-<a href="#S6.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 6.3 Empirical Results: Learning Experts from Training Data for MoE with Uniform Segmentation for One-Dimensional Inputs ‣ 6 Learning the Constant Experts: A Tradeoff in the Number of Experts ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> show the differences between the learned constants (in the learned predictors, shown as solid black lines) and the optimal constants (in the best in-class nonlearned predictors, computed by (<a href="#S4.E15" title="In 4 Approximation Error of MoE for One-Dimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) for the uniform segmentation, and shown as dotted magenta lines).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figs. <a href="#S6.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 6.3 Empirical Results: Learning Experts from Training Data for MoE with Uniform Segmentation for One-Dimensional Inputs ‣ 6 Learning the Constant Experts: A Tradeoff in the Number of Experts ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <a href="#S6.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 6.3 Empirical Results: Learning Experts from Training Data for MoE with Uniform Segmentation for One-Dimensional Inputs ‣ 6 Learning the Constant Experts: A Tradeoff in the Number of Experts ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> show the approximation error curves for the model class <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{m,1}^{c}\left(\{a_{i}=\frac{i}{m}\}_{i=1}^{m}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{m,1}^{c}\left(\{a_{i}=\frac{i}{m}\}_{i=1}^{m}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. There is an excellent match between the empirical evaluation (on a test dataset of 5000 examples) and the numerical evaluation of the theoretical error formula from (<a href="#S4.E21" title="In Corollary 2. ‣ 4.2 Optimizing the Routing Segmentation using Expert Segment Density ‣ 4 Approximation Error of MoE for One-Dimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>) that is suitable also for suboptimal segment densities, as we have here with the uniform segmentation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Figs. <a href="#S6.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 6.3 Empirical Results: Learning Experts from Training Data for MoE with Uniform Segmentation for One-Dimensional Inputs ‣ 6 Learning the Constant Experts: A Tradeoff in the Number of Experts ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <a href="#S6.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 6.3 Empirical Results: Learning Experts from Training Data for MoE with Uniform Segmentation for One-Dimensional Inputs ‣ 6 Learning the Constant Experts: A Tradeoff in the Number of Experts ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> show the test error curves for three different sizes of training dataset. These test error values were computed empirically as the average over 300 experiments, each with another randomly drawn training dataset. The test error curves qualitatively demonstrate the tradeoff between the approximation error and the estimation error as function of the number of experts <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, as discussed in Section <a href="#S6.SS2" title="6.2 Test Error Analysis for MoE with Learned Expert Constants ‣ 6 Learning the Constant Experts: A Tradeoff in the Number of Experts ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a>. Specifically, the test error curve first decreases with <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, due to the significant decrease of the approximation error that dominates the overall test error trend for sufficiently small <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>; then, the test error curve increases with <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, due to the increase of the estimation error that dominates the overall test error trend for sufficiently large <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. It can be observed that the minimal test error is obtained for a larger <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> if the training dataset is larger; this is because having more training data increases the probability of having more training examples in each region, which in return allows the regions to be smaller without increasing the estimation error too much.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F3" class="ltx_figure"><span id="S6.F3.2" class="ltx_ERROR ltx_centering undefined">\subfigure</span>
<br class="ltx_break ltx_break">
<p id="S6.F3.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">[]
<img src="2510.03151v1/example1_learning_given_uniform_segmentation__beta_reconstruction_and_segmentation_m18.png" id="S6.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:181/135;" width="181" height="135" alt="Refer to caption">


<span id="S6.F3.3.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\subfigure</span>[]
<img src="2510.03151v1/example1_learning_given_uniform_segmentation__beta_reconstruction_and_segmentation_m50.png" id="S6.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:181/136;" width="181" height="136" alt="Refer to caption">



<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S6.F3.3.2" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\subfigure</span>[]
<img src="2510.03151v1/example1_learning_given_uniform_segmentation__approximation_error_curves.png" id="S6.F3.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:181/136;" width="181" height="136" alt="Refer to caption">


<span id="S6.F3.3.3" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\subfigure</span>[]
<img src="2510.03151v1/example1_learning_given_uniform_segmentation__test_error_curves.png" id="S6.F3.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:181/136;" width="181" height="136" alt="Refer to caption"></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Examples for <span id="S6.F3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">learning experts for uniform segmentation</span>. This experiment is for a <span id="S6.F3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">cosine</span> <math id="S6.F3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and truncated Gaussian <math id="S6.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> from Figs. <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 4.4 Empirical Results: Approximation Error, Optimal Segmentation and Experts ‣ 4 Approximation Error of MoE for One-Dimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 4.4 Empirical Results: Approximation Error, Optimal Segmentation and Experts ‣ 4 Approximation Error of MoE for One-Dimensional Inputs ‣ Mixture of Many Zero-Compute Experts:A High-Rate Quantization Theory Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, respectively.
Here, in (a)-(b), the red markers on the <math id="S6.F3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-axis denote the given uniform segmentation points <math id="S6.F3.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{a_{i}=\frac{i}{m}\}_{i=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{i}=\frac{i}{m}\}_{i=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>; the dotted magenta lines show the best predictor in <math id="S6.F3.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{m,1}^{c}\left(\{a_{i}=\frac{i}{m}\}_{i=1}^{m}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{m,1}^{c}\left(\{a_{i}=\frac{i}{m}\}_{i=1}^{m}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>; the black lines show the predictor learned from a training data of 200 examples.
(c) shows the empirical and theoretical approximation error curves of the best predictors in <math id="S6.F3.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{m,1}^{c}\left(\{a_{i}=\frac{i}{m}\}_{i=1}^{m}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{m,1}^{c}\left(\{a_{i}=\frac{i}{m}\}_{i=1}^{m}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.
(d) shows the empirical test error curves for the learned predictors for three sizes of training dataset.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S6.F4" class="ltx_figure"><span id="S6.F4.2" class="ltx_ERROR ltx_centering undefined">\subfigure</span>
<br class="ltx_break ltx_break">
<p id="S6.F4.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">[]
<img src="2510.03151v1/example2_learning_given_uniform_segmentation__beta_reconstruction_and_segmentation_m18.png" id="S6.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:181/136;" width="181" height="136" alt="Refer to caption">


<span id="S6.F4.3.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\subfigure</span>[]
